{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794425451,"nonce":"AIyV4quY9uX273ZZWAJHFiyS2IM=","title":"Reported 2026-11-12 from the devops-gitops pilot on Scarab 0.3.2, who asked whether `build.image` accepts `${{ event.sha }}`. Read from the code; not run.","message":"It does not, and nothing says so. Rendering covers a Step's `image`, `command` and `env` only (`render_launch_spec`, crates/scarab-engine/src/render.rs:38-76; `interpolatable_strings`, crates/scarab-pipeline/src/lib.rs:4233). `BuildSpec.image` is never rendered, and compile-time validation of a build step checks only that `image` is non-empty (lib.rs:5561). So\n\n    build:\n      image: 123.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/ci-tools:${{ event.sha }}\n      push: true\n\ncompiles, and buildctl receives `name=…/ci-tools:${{ event.sha }}` as text and fails at run time.\n\nFix:\n1. Render `build.image` (and `context`, `dockerfile`) through the same renderer as a Step's `image`, with the same scope rules. `event.sha` is a fixed-shape field and is safe in a tag.\n2. After rendering, check that `build.image` is an OCI image reference (`crate::oci::is_image_reference`, as render does for `image`), and refuse at launch otherwise.\n3. Until 1 lands, at least reject `${{` in any `build.*` field at compile time.\n\nDocs, same ticket: a multi-arch recipe. Today it works with plain steps: one `build:` step per architecture with `placement_profiles` choosing an amd64 or arm64 node pool (`apply_placement` runs on build Pods too, executor-k8s lib.rs:2790), each pushing `…:\u003csha\u003e-amd64` / `…:\u003csha\u003e-arm64`, then an ordinary step that merges them (`crane index append` or `docker buildx imagetools create`) with its own registry login. That recipe needs fix 1 for the per-SHA tags.\n\nLater, not this ticket: a `platforms:` field on `build:` that fans out per platform and adds the merge step.\n\nAcceptance: `${{ event.sha }}` in `build.image` reaches buildctl as the SHA; a free-text field there is refused like it is in `image`; a malformed rendered reference is refused at launch; the docs page has the multi-arch recipe.","files":null}]}